  "sources": [
    "style-research ch.1 §7 Memphis / Phonk",
    "style-research ch.1 Cross-genre engine constants",
    "Splice — \"What is Memphis rap?\" (DR-5 / TR-808 / MPC60 / SP-1200 kits, cowbell and clave percussion)"
  ],
  "notes": "The tape-era ancestor phonk was built out of, and the sparse one. Two or three kicks a bar under a snare on the 3, hats thin and triplet-flavoured rather than a carpet, and a cowbell riding the bar as a voice rather than a garnish — the clave answering it. No clap stack: the snare stands on its own, which is most of why a Memphis loop sounds naked next to a drift-phonk one. The 808 is a long subby boom layered onto the kick one-shot and it barely moves — no octave glides. Harmony is a one- or two-chord phrygian vamp in triads, and the melody is a low four-note loop with the flat second in it.",
